A Mathematical Programming Model for Minimizing Energy Consumption on Selective Laser Melting Machine
Junjie Lin1 and Chunlong Yu2


Abstract— The scheduling problem of additive manufacturing is receiving increasing attention, yet few have considered the effect of scheduling decisions on the machine energy consumption. This research focuses on the nesting and scheduling problem of a single selective laser melting (SLM) machine to reduce the total energy consumption. Based on an energy consumption model, a nesting and scheduling problem is formulated, and a mixed integer linear programming model is proposed. This model determines simultaneously the part-tobatch assignments, part placement in the batch and the choice of build orientation to reduce the total energy consumption of the SLM machine. The energy-saving potential of the model is validated through numerical experiments. Moreover, the effect of number of alternative build orientations on the energy consumption is explored.
Keywords: 3D printing, SLM, energy saving, scheduling, mathematical programming
I. INTRODUCTION
Compared with conventional manufacturing, additive manufacturing (AM) has a great potential to improve material efficiency, reduce lifecycle impact, and enable greater engineering capabilities (Huang et al., 2016). Despite several advantages, AM has been recognized as an energy-intensive technology, especially in large-scale parts and mass production. The formation of multiple thin layers from raw materials requires a considerable amount of energy, and thus the energy consumption per unit volume of material is often much higher than conventional manufacturing techniques (Yoon et al., 2014). This aspect raises concerns about the potential negative economical and environmental impacts and therefore, reducing energy consumption is becoming one of the most important goals driving the adoption of additive manufacturing in key industries (Karimi et al., 2021).
The research of energy aware scheduling in manufacturing systems is receiving considerable attention. Various studies have considered the multi-objective scheduling problem and proposed methods to optimize energy cost with other common scheduling criteria (Jia et al., 2017; Zhou et al., 2018; Wang et al., 2018). In these studies, the energy saving opportunities come from a better allocation of jobs to machines with different speeds and powers, as well as a better job sequence to avoid high consumption in the periods of high electricity price.
However, few works have considered the scheduling problem that aims at reducing energy consumption in AM machines. In this research, we focus on saving the energy

