C. Power of subsystem
The power parameters of each subsystem are summarized in Table 1. The values of each parameter is machine dependent and can be measured by the power acquisition device, including data acquisition chassis, data acquisition card, voltage sensor, current sensor, etc. For details, please refer to Lv et al. (2021). Note that the power of the laser subsystem is different during scanning border, filling contour, volume hatching and creating supports, for convenience, we treat the laser used in these sub-processes as four different subsystems, thus the total energy consumption can be obtained by Eq. (1).
D. State of subsystems
Generally, a subsystem of a SLM machine can be fully on, off, or running intermittently in different sub-processes. For example, the platform heater is fully on during the preheating phase to raise the temperature of platform to the required level, while during the laser exposure phase it runs intermittently to keep the temperature constant. Therefore, to estimate correctly the energy consumption of each subsystem,

it is critical to identify their actual operating states during each sub-process.
To this end, the working state factor 𝑘 is introduced. For the 𝑓-th subsystem, 𝑘 is the ratio of the actual running time to the total time of the 𝑙-th sub-process As shown in Figure 2, the basic subsystem and water circulation unit are running all the time, whilst the laser, powder spreading motor, electric valve and gas circulation pump motor only operate at certain stages. In these cases, the 𝑘 is either 1 or 0. The platform heater and water cooling unit operate intermittently. To decide the values of 𝑘 for these two subsystems, measurements are often required. See Lv et al. (2021) for details of the measurement procedure.
