C. Experiment setup
With the above settings, we have conducted two experiments：
1. The first numerical experiment compares the energy consumption difference between MILP and a commercial 3D printing software Magics. We have total six kinds of parts, named part1-6, 4 copies for parts 1 and 2, and 3 copies for parts 3 to 6. Magics uses the default build orientation for each part, which is the smallest Z-axis height. For MILP, we assume that each part has five different alternative orientations, which are provided by the Magics software: building orientation 1 is the orientation rendering the minimum part height. Building orientation 2 corresponds to

the minimum support area, that is, the support structure occupies the smallest projection area on the build platform. Building orientation 3 is the one with the smallest XY section. That is, when using the section vertical to the height direction to cut the part, the maximum XY section of the part is the smallest. Build orientation 4 is the XY projection, that is, the projected area of the part on the XY plane is the smallest. Build direction 5 is to generate support in the marked area. Then, we collated, analyzed and compared batch information for both nesting, including part volume, part surface area, volume of support structure, batch height, as well as printing time and printing energy consumption. The MILP model is solved by the Gurobi solver(ver 9.5.2) with a maximum CPU runtime of 7200s.
2. In experiment 2, a design of experiments is performed with the following factors and levels: number of parts – {20, 25, 30}, number of alternative build orientations – {1,3,5,7}. This results in 12 experiments. We analyze the impact of numbers of parts and alternative build orientations on the energy consumption of the MILP model.
