III. VII. CONCLUSION
In this study, we study the nesting and scheduling problem on a single SLM machine to reduce the energy consumption. We take into account the 2D nesting subproblem when packing the parts into batches as well as the fact that a part has multiple optional build orientations. We propose a mathematical programming model and several improvements to strengthen the model.
By comparing the results of solving the MILP model to that provided by a commercial 3D printing software, we demonstrate the energy saving potential of solving the scheduling problem in 3D printing and reveals the reasons of

energy savings. More specifically, when the build orientation of the parts is fixed, the MILP model can reduce the height of batches by distributing the parts to batches in a proper way, thereby reducing the final energy consumption. When the parts have multiple optional build orientations, the MILP model can obtain a greater energy saving. This is due to the increased flexibility that can be made use for energy saving. However, increasing the number of build orientations also boosts the difficulty of solving the model accordingly, and the trade-off has to be made.
As shown by the numerical results, there is still room for improving the energy saving by increasing the efficiency of the model. Future works will focus on developing efficient solution approaches such as matheuristics and metaheuristics to tackle large-scale problems. In addition, since the multimachine environment is more in line with the industrial practice, an extension to the multi-machine scheduling problem is also one of our future studies.
